// std/v3_l1.dag — substrate marker types for v3's L1 behaviors and
// the meta-type sentinel `Declaration`.
//
// Two distinct purposes live in this file:
//
// 1. **L1 behavior markers.** Every Behavior variant in
//    `src/v3/compiler/src/dag.rs` (Value, Transform, Branch, Loop,
//    Bind) plus the Main-wrapper concept needs a structural anchor
//    that per-target language spec files can reference. Without
//    these markers, target spec files would have to identify
//    behaviors by string name (e.g. `target_name: "Bind"`), which
//    pushes the dispatch keys into Rust strings on the emitter
//    side. With markers, the spec files reference a typed
//    Declaration, the lowerer resolves the reference to a
//    DeclarationId, and the emitter uses that DeclarationId as the
//    realization-index key. Zero name strings cross the
//    substrate/emitter boundary.
//
// 2. **`Declaration` sentinel meta-type.** A field type that means
//    "any declaration reference." When a record-literal field's
//    declared type walks to this sentinel, the lowerer accepts an
//    identifier (or dotted path) as the field value and emits
//    `FieldValue::Reference(decl_id)` instead of
//    `FieldValue::Literal(...)`. This is what unlocks target spec
//    files writing `target: Int` (a typed reference to the Int
//    declaration in std/integer.dag) instead of
//    `target_name: "Int"` (a string the emitter has to dispatch
//    on).
//
// **Why these live in src/v3/spec/ rather than dsl/std/.** v2's
// CI pipeline scans `dsl/` recursively and tries to resolve every
// identifier in every record-literal field value. v2 has no
// concept of v3's `Declaration` sentinel meta-type, so a target
// spec file like rust.dag carrying `target: Int` (a typed
// declaration reference) reads as an undefined-variable error in
// v2's scope. Moving v3-only spec files to `src/v3/spec/` keeps
// them outside v2's scan tree entirely. v3 reads them via
// `include_str!` from `src/v3/compiler/src/bootstrap.rs`.

module v3.spec.v3_l1

// L1 behavior markers. One per Behavior variant in dag.rs plus
// Main for the program-entry-point wrapper. Each marker is an
// empty Conj — it carries no fields because its only role is to
// be the typed handle that realizations point at.
type Value {}
type Transform {}
type Branch {}
type Loop {}
type Bind {}
type Main {}

// Sentinel meta-type. A field declared `target: Declaration`
// accepts an identifier value instead of a literal value at the
// data-body parse site, and that identifier is resolved to a
// DeclarationId during lowering. This is the structural
// alternative to passing declaration names as strings — the
// substrate carries the typed reference forward, no name-bridge
// lookup at consumption time.
type Declaration {}
